          guideText: `{name}. We have spent five lessons on your own mind. Now we turn outward to the hardest question about living together: what makes a society just? Picture a few hundred strangers who have to share one island — who have different wants, different strengths, different ideas of right and wrong. How should they organize themselves? Who, if anyone, should be in charge? How much freedom should each person have, and when can the group limit it? How should the good things and the hard things be shared out? These are the questions of political philosophy, and they are not abstract: every law, every government, every argument about fairness you will ever hear is really an argument about these. Here is what I want you to notice up front, because it matters in our noisy age: these are genuine questions with several serious, defensible answers, given by brilliant people who deeply disagreed — not a contest between obviously-right and obviously-evil sides. My job today is not to tell you what to think about politics; it is to hand you the great questions and the great answers fairly, and let you do your own thinking. In fact, this is the perfect place to practice everything you just learned about not collapsing into us-versus-them certainty. Let's think about how to live together.`,
