              audioPrompt: `One modern thinker offered a tool for thinking about fairness so clever it is worth knowing on its own, {name}, whatever you ultimately conclude. The American philosopher John Rawls, writing in the 1900s, asked a disarmingly simple question: how would we design a society if we wanted it to be genuinely fair to everyone, not just to people like us? His answer was a thought experiment called the veil of ignorance. Here is how it works. Imagine you have to design the rules of a society, how wealth is shared, what rights people have, how power works, but you have to do it from behind a veil that hides one crucial thing: you do not know who you will be in that society. You might be rich or poor, healthy or sick, talented or struggling, born into any family or group. You could end up the most fortunate person, or the least. Rawls's insight is that designing rules from behind that veil pushes you toward genuine fairness almost automatically, because if you do not know whether you will land at the top or the bottom, you will not design a society that crushes those at the bottom; you would not risk being one of them. You would build in real protections for the worst-off, because that person might be you. It is the philosophical version of "you cut the cake, I choose my slice." What makes it so powerful is that it strips away the self-interest that usually distorts our thinking about fairness. In real life, people's sense of what is fair tends to line up suspiciously well with what benefits people like themselves. The veil cuts through that. You do not have to accept every conclusion Rawls drew, and serious thinkers have pushed back on where exactly it leads. But the veil of ignorance is a genuinely useful tool anyone, of any political leaning, can use: whatever rule you favor, ask whether you would still favor it if you did not know which person in society you were going to be.`,
